Optional helper -- most builders wire ghost.o themselves (append
`obj-y += ghost.o` to the Makefile of whichever directory they drop ghost.c in).
This hunk is the fs/proc variant: copy ghost.c AND ghost.h into fs/proc/
(co-located, so ghost.c's #include "ghost.h" resolves), then apply.

fs/proc/ rather than fs/ for two reasons: it is where _pathhide already puts
pathhide.c, so a builder that ships both copies four files into one directory;
and fs/Makefile is edited by the nomount engine patches, while fs/proc/Makefile
is not.

Deliberately unconditional obj-y (no CONFIG_GHOST), for the same reason
_pathhide's build hunk is: a new symbol in /proc/config.gz would out the patch
to anything that reads it, which is precisely what this patch set exists to
prevent. ghost.c is inert until configured, so an unconditional build costs a
few hundred bytes of text and PATH_MAX per CPU, and changes no behaviour.

ANCHORED AWAY FROM _pathhide'S HUNK ON PURPOSE. pathhide_build_integration.patch
inserts at `obj-y += proc.o` (lines 6-7); this one inserts after
`proc-$(CONFIG_MMU) := task_mmu.o` (lines 9-10). The two never overlap, so they
apply in either order -- the second one applied just picks up an offset of 1,
which `patch -F0` accepts (offset is not fuzz). Verified both orders.

The anchor lines are byte-identical in fs/proc/Makefile on v4.9 through
torvalds/master and on all five pinned OnePlus trees.
--- a/fs/proc/Makefile
+++ b/fs/proc/Makefile
@@ -9,6 +9,8 @@
 proc-y			:= nommu.o task_nommu.o
 proc-$(CONFIG_MMU)	:= task_mmu.o
 
+obj-y			+= ghost.o
+
 proc-y       += inode.o root.o base.o generic.o array.o \
 		fd.o
 proc-$(CONFIG_TTY)      += proc_tty.o
